Aliases & Set Operations for Reporting (Regional Sales Ops)

Objective


Master column/table aliases and set operations to build clean, unified reports from multiple regional sources. You’ll practice:

Column & table aliases for readability
UNION / UNION ALL (and how to dedupe)
INTERSECT & MINUS / EXCEPT use-cases with portable patterns (since MySQL doesn’t natively support these, you’ll learn JOIN-based equivalents)
Practical reporting patterns: harmonizing schemas, tagging sources, sorting, and post-merge calculations
DB: MySQL 8+.
Note on portability: PostgreSQL/SQL Server support INTERSECT and EXCEPT. MySQL does not—we’ll use JOIN-based equivalents you can port anywhere.

Case Study — “NorthStar Retail”


NorthStar Retail consolidates Quarterly Sales from North, South, and West regions. Legacy processes left small schema differences. You’ll:

Normalize column names with aliases,
Combine datasets into a single report using UNION / UNION ALL,
Compute shared customers across regions (INTERSECT-like), and
Find customers exclusive to a region (MINUS/EXCEPT-like).

Part A — Setup & Seed Data


Step 1 — Create and enter the database


CREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS northstar_retail;
USE northstar_retail;

Step 2 — Create region tables (slight schema differences by design)


DROP TABLE IF EXISTS sales_north, sales_south, sales_west, customers;

CREATE TABLE customers (
  customer_id INT PRIMARY KEY,
  customer_name VARCHAR(80),
  segment VARCHAR(40)      -- e.g., Consumer, Corporate
);

CREATE TABLE sales_north (
  sale_id INT PRIMARY KEY,
  customer_id INT,
  product VARCHAR(80),
  qty INT,
  net_amount DECIMAL(10,2),
  sale_date DATE,
  FOREIGN KEY (customer_id) REFERENCES customers(customer_id)
);

-- South region calls 'amount' instead of 'net_amount'
CREATE TABLE sales_south (
  sale_id INT PRIMARY KEY,
  customer_id INT,
  product VARCHAR(80),
  qty INT,
  amount DECIMAL(10,2),
  sale_date DATE,
  FOREIGN KEY (customer_id) REFERENCES customers(customer_id)
);

-- West region uses different column order and names
CREATE TABLE sales_west (
  id INT PRIMARY KEY,
  cust_id INT,
  item VARCHAR(80),
  quantity INT,
  gross DECIMAL(10,2),
  sold_on DATE,
  FOREIGN KEY (cust_id) REFERENCES customers(customer_id)
);

